 was not to privatize the goal was to be  as efficient and effective as possible  sometimes that meant you continue to do  things the way you always done not very  often but sometimes other times it meant  you had privatize the asset other times  it means you set up manage competition  so labor would have to bid on continue  to do the work they've done  become more productive I generally found  that if we very sophisticated test open  up the phonebook and the yellow pages if  there are three or more other businesses  in the same area in your city you're  probably not the most efficient provider  of those services and we bit out or did  manage competition over eighty times you  know many cities today in America  obviously are you know facing structural  deficits are they following the the path  that you blazed in Indianapolis and if  not what what's the biggest problem is  it not knowing how to do that or is it  political interest getting in the water  I think the basic problem is an  understanding of the goal right the goal  isn't to monetize assets so you bring  all of the revenues forward like parking  meters and then spend it on operating  the goal is efficiency right so how do  you operate something more efficiently  is there anybody in the world better  than you are in your city at operating  streetlights or parking our golf courses  or water or wastewater or airports then  you take the savings and invest that in  future to say so I think the thing  that's holding people back is that  they're thinking about it as do we want  to privatize do we want to sell the  family jewels as contrasted you what is  the role of government in terms of  managing that asset was a specific asset  or thing that Indianapolis was doing and  how did you shift it into a more  efficient mode right so we did about 80  of these either managed competition or  privatization we did the first large  wastewater privatization in the country  you know today there are tens of  billions of dollars of mandates against  local and authorities for water and  wastewater EPA compliance and the like  so the city needs to clean its water  right there are environmental  responsibilities Indianapolis at the  time according to Ernst and Young was  one of the most efficiently run  wastewater treatment plants in the  country and yet then we went ahead and  asked for anybody in the world to give  us a bit about how to manage our plan  and it turns out that the winning bidder  had more PhDs and we had employs right  there was no way that we were as  effective as they were there's no way we  had the best technology the way we had  the best management so essentially then  they managed the plants for about 25 to  30 percent less per year which produced  a you know a savings that we could then  use  debt or efficiency purposes for some  other reason to invest it in the  infrastructure of the city are there any  big mistakes that you made where either  the privatization or failing to do that  just blew out right if you continue to  do a mediocre service and don't change  the status quo you generally don't get  criticized if you try to change the  status quo let's say it's like this and  you go like this that you wanted to go  like this it's often viewed as a failure  still better than it was before but not  as good so one of the most important  lessons we learned was benchmark  everything before you begin benchmark in  terms of quality and benchmarking in  terms of cost and occasionally we  slipped up on that the other issue which  I don't think is a mistake but we did  make mistakes and not a mistake in terms  of we shouldn't have done it is what are  the service level requirements after you  privatize the asset right what do you  want the experience to be for passengers  in the airport what do you want to and  the quality of the water to be how high  do you want the grass to be after you  privatize the mowing of the medians so  get the service levels right get the  benchmarks where you have the numbers  right and then you can do it correctly  and occasionally got those wrong let's  talk about the next frontiers in public  private partnerships or making I mean  actually it's more making government  more efficient how does the responsive  City your 2014 book deal with that or  where what what is that about so I think  we're in a really interesting moment  where public-private partnerships could  blossom in a pretty dramatic way so if  we have technologies that are highly  refined let's say go back to water and  waste water so it puts sensors in pipes  we know where the leaks are we know  where the pressure is we know when the  water hydrant is going to be low you  know we can anticipate a problem and fix  it before it occurs we have a sensor on  a bridge it says this bridge is  vibrating too much in Minneapolis but  you know it's going to fall down if you  don't come fix it right a streetlight  that says I'm about to burn out come fix  me before I burn out right so the  public-private partnerships and what we  talked about in responsive City is how  to use data how to use data analytics  how to use sensors in order to  anticipate problems and solve them  before they become serious what are the  chief impediments to pushing forward on  using big data  on using you know pushing this forward  where is where's the resistance the  impediments generally are perceived to  be is this hostile to labor and our  experience was it wasn't hostile to  labor at all in fact our labor as they  became more productive earn more money  what we said is we don't need as many  managers and we need more technology so  as we look at the uses of technology  we're having a pretty high attrition and  retirement rate in the public service  right because of the age of the average  public employee so you don't have to  layoff anyone we're we're basically  saying to employees is as a result of  partnering with the private sector as a  result of getting more technology 